From 00f12354cf295a6dc3728872b114ba62840440cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jetson <jetson@orin-nano.tail52323b.ts.net>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:58:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] nv_attach: sum-map routing fixes after review
 (fail-closed definitions, no-copy maps, 64-bit adds)

- Register definitions are counted per statement and fail closed: any
  register in an instruction's destination position (a {..} vector, a call's
  (..) return, a p|q pair, after a label or a spaced guard, several
  statements on a line) counts, stores, reductions and branches excepted.
  The line-based test missed those forms, so a register also written there
  could be taken for a sum map's address and its atomic made .global.
- A constant map fd is a register written by one statement, a mov of an
  integer (the opcode list let neg, not, rem, vector loads through).
- A sum map without a device copy keeps the trampoline's lookup: a copy made
  later is found through the run-time map_info, where a cached patched image
  would have kept an inlined NULL.
- Only 64-bit adds take the global fast path (the map's documented use);
  the address offset is added before cvta.to.global.
- Tests: whole-block order of the lookup and the add, the missed definition
  forms, non-constant fds, 32-bit and non-add atomics, kernels left alone and
  helpers given 0 without a thread-local map, routed with one.
pt_victim's five tools exact.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
